libvirt-backup (0.1.5) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Locking rework for clusters sharing one backup directory: the
    single global lock is replaced by a per-host run lock
    (.libvirt-backup-<host>.lock) plus a per-VM lock
    (.libvirt-backup-vm-<vm>.lock) taken around each VM's backup and
    by directory-mode restores.  All hosts of a cluster can now back
    up concurrently; a VM live-migrated while its backup still runs
    on the old host is skipped by the new host (SKIP, holder named)
    instead of racing for the same '<vm>-backup-*' artefacts, chain
    file and snapshots.  Lock files record '<host> <pid>'; remote
    holders are reported as-is.  Repository mode needs no extra
    coordination: rustic is lock-free by design and restic's backup
    lock is non-exclusive (only prune/check take an exclusive one).

 -- Thomas Kotzian <thomas.kotzian@lausa.at>  Sat, 07 Nov 2026 12:20:00 +0100

libvirt-backup (0.1.4) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Selection overhaul: every run now backs up the VM whose last
    successful backup lies furthest back first, re-evaluated before
    every VM.  Interrupted long runs continue where they stopped
    instead of restarting alphabetically, and a live-migrated VM is
    seen with its real history on the receiving host.  Repository
    mode reads the last success from the run tags, directory mode
    from the new per-VM <vm>-state.json.
  * New 'backoff' option (--backoff / [backup] backoff, e.g. "7d" or
    seconds): minimum time between two backups of the same VM;
    -D selections bypass it.
  * Per-VM failure tracking in <vm>-state.json: after more than 2
    consecutive failures the administrator is notified and the VM is
    only retried once the backoff period has passed since the last
    error; any success resets the counters.

 -- Thomas Kotzian <thomas.kotzian@lausa.at>  Wed, 28 Oct 2026 23:55:00 +0100

libvirt-backup (0.1.3) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Security: the tool now runs with umask 0077 - backup images,
    restored files, <vm>-config.xml (dumpxml can contain VNC
    passwords), logs and chain state were previously created with the
    caller's umask, i.e. usually world-readable VM disk images;
    warnings when the config file or the repository password file is
    accessible to group/others; string config values (uri,
    backup_dir, repository, password_file, S3 keys) are validated.
  * Robustness: SIGINT/SIGHUP/SIGTERM now abort the current VM
    cleanly (fsThaw the guest, abort the backup job, discard the
    overlay) and stop the run with a summary, instead of dying
    silently between fsFreeze and fsThaw and leaving the guest file
    systems frozen; corrupt incremental chain files fall back to a
    new full backup; 'restore -d <file>' and empty qemu-img output
    produce clean errors.
  * 'check' in directory mode finally does what the documentation
    promised: qemu-img check every standalone image in backup_dir
    (chain members are skipped with a reason, raw images listed).
  * systemd services gain NoNewPrivileges and PrivateTmp.

 -- Thomas Kotzian <thomas.kotzian@lausa.at>  Wed, 28 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0100

libvirt-backup (0.1.2) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Streaming robustness: qemu-storage-daemon and rustic/restic
    stderr now go to log files instead of unread pipes; a chatty
    client could fill the 64 KiB pipe buffer and silently deadlock a
    multi-hour stream.  Error details are still reported from the
    log files; the libnbd handle is closed explicitly.
  * One VM's unexpected error (OSError, ValueError, ...) no longer
    aborts the whole run: the VM is reported FAIL with the message
    and the remaining VMs are still backed up; a blockJobInfo query
    failure (domain destroyed mid-backup) becomes a clear error.
  * Config validation: every [backup]/[repo]/[domains.*] value is
    checked at load time; quoted numbers, string booleans
    ('no' was truthy), invalid per-domain dest_format and
    compress/dest_format/standalone contradictions now abort with
    file, key and value instead of tracebacks or silent inversion;
    malformed JSON from the repository client is reported as such.

 -- Thomas Kotzian <thomas.kotzian@lausa.at>  Wed, 28 Oct 2026 09:30:00 +0100

libvirt-backup (0.1.1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Robustness release, all around the backup run:
    - checkpoints are only attempted for directory-mode VMs with
      incremental enabled (fixes a repo-mode crash on qcow2 VMs and
      removes WARN noise for raw/iSCSI VMs)
    - leftover libvirt checkpoints are reported as WARN before each
      backup and in --dry-run, with a virsh checkpoint-delete hint
    - whole-run flock on the backup directory: concurrent backups skip
      (exit 0), directory restores from a busy source refuse; --force
      overrides a stuck lock, holder PID and fuser diagnostics included
    - restic mode: stale repository locks are cleared automatically via
      'restic unlock' + retry; rustic's lock-free design documented
    - pre-flight before the first block job: repository probe (missing
      init, wrong password/region/keys) and writable backup dir
    - stale .qcow2.job intermediates from aborted runs are discarded
      automatically; a still-active backup job fails the VM with a
      virsh job-abort hint; failed runs no longer leave overlays behind
  * Code quality: ruff format + ruff check (E,F,W,B) compliance,
    docstrings for all functions.

 -- Thomas Kotzian <thomas.kotzian@lausa.at>  Wed, 28 Oct 2026 08:00:00 +0100

libvirt-backup (0.1.0) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Initial packaged release: libvirt push backup/restore tool with
    directory and rustic/restic repository modes, retention
    (forget), integrity check, repository init, and guarded systemd
    timers.
  * S3 with rustic >= 0.8 is routed through rustic's opendal backend
    automatically (the native s3 backend was removed there).

 -- Thomas Kotzian <thomas.kotzian@lausa.at>  Tue, 27 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0100
